  "worked_solution": "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta +k\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-3,-1),$ $a=4$ and $b=2.$  Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta -3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta -1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a half-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the line parallel to the $x$-axis and crossing the center of the ellipse.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065811/assets/4659fda20ed9a2b5e5c10d60f504e4db378efb0bfe9ae037f4998511e75300cb.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\pi ]$, we get the half-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the half-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta -3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta -1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
